WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.660 --> 00:01:46.610
DOZIER: So welcome to today's Council on Foreign
Relations meeting on "Women in the Armed Forces:

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.590
The Future of the Military."

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:53.420
We're starting things promptly at 8:30 and
1/2.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:59.530
Today we have with us Juliet Beyler, who is
the principal director of force resiliency

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.869
of the Office of the Under Secretary of Defense
for Personnel and Readiness at the Pentagon.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:09.500
Gayle Tzemach Lemmon; she's the senior fellow
for women and foreign policy at CFR and author

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:15.920
of "Ashley's War: The Untold Story of a Team
of Women Soldiers on the Special Ops Battlefield,"

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.570
just now out in paperback.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:23.920
And she said that three of the women from
the book are in the room, so we will be putting

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.160
you on the spot later.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.940
And we also have Agnes Gereben Schaefer, who
is a senior political scientist at the RAND

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:36.080
Corporation and has done many studies on the
issue at hand.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:41.830
So, we start with the first question.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:48.500
Last December, Secretary of Defense Ash Carter
opened all combat roles to women.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.500
Can you give us an update?

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.500
Where does it stand?

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.500
BEYLER: Sure, absolutely.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.500
Thank you.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.500
DOZIER: Bringing policy into action.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.500
BEYLER: Yes, absolutely.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:55.600
So, good morning, everyone.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.090
Thank you for having me.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:02.330
So, as Kimberly said, so the secretary announced
in early December that he was opening all

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.570
remaining positions to women.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:08.870
And during that announcement, he directed
that the services kind of develop detailed

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.820
implementation plans to come back to him by
the 1st of January, explaining exactly-articulating

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.930
exactly the details on how they were going
to make this happen.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:20.690
We stood up an implementation group that was
co-chaired by the deputy secretary of defense

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:25.820
and the vice chairman of the Joint Chiefs
of Staff, and they combed through-we had several

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.880
meetings with the vice chiefs and the leadership
of the department, looking through each of

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:30.880
the services.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.720
They cross-briefed each other on the details
of the plan, looking to make sure that they-there

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.740
were no issues, and that everything was covered,
and that they had all addressed all of the

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.570
secretary's concerns that were laid out in
his December memo.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.630
Then, in 9 March, the secretary, we briefed
those up to him.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:51.459
He reviewed all of the plans personally himself,
and he approved all of the plans on 9 March.

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:57.069
And he said everyone go forth and open everything
no later than April 1st, which we are past

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.069
that date.

00:03:58.069 --> 00:04:02.770
Positions are open, and people are starting
to assess and recruit and assign women.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.319
And so that's where we stand today.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:05.490
DOZIER: Got it.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:09.880
So, I guess the shorter version of that question
is, when are we going to see a woman Navy

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:10.880
SEAL?

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:11.880
(Laughter.)

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:12.880
BEYLER: (Chuckles.)

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.100
Well, so the short answer to that is probably
in-at the absolute earliest the summer of

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.100
2018.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:24.850
So the SEAL pipeline is very long, so the
next enlisted SEAL course doesn't start until

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.090
this summer, and the officer SEAL course later
in the year.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.410
So, of course, the assessment and selection
process is very long to even get into the

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.410
pipeline.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.970
And then, given the length of the training,
it takes about two years.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:40.480
So if we have a woman in one of the first
two classes, you still won't even see the

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.800
first one until '18.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.900
DOZIER: And same for Rangers?

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:50.390
BEYLER: So, the-as this group knows, the Ranger
course is significantly shorter.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:57.370
So there are-so the Army did recruit their
first female enlisted infantry woman.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:58.370
She signed up.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.990
She'll go to-she'll go to the course later
this summer.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:01.990
It depends.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.780
So, for instance, the academies, when they
graduate, there are a number of women, both

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:09.560
at West Point and at the Naval Academy, who
have identified that they're interested in

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.050
possibly going into these career tracks.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:15.760
So, of course, they'll get commissioned here
shortly, and then they will go to their courses.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.660
So each course is different, based on the
service and the occupation.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:22.680
DOZIER: I taught at the Army War College last
year, so if I didn't ask about the Army I'd

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.440
be in trouble.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:31.850
So, but we were talking about beforehand that
there's a certain fiction that women haven't

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:37.470
already been in combat, because in the past
decade-plus war they have.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:43.991
And, Gayle, you said you just covered some
remarks that-where they-where the commander

00:05:43.991 --> 00:05:45.000
brought that out.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.400
LEMMON: Yeah, so, first off, good morning.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.210
I'm really delighted to be here and to have
all of you here in an early morning.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:53.210
So thank you.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.460
Thanks to this incredible panel.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:02.060
And really, for me, from a storytelling perspective,
it's been a huge privilege and really a journey

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:07.750
to bring a story about which I was entirely
ignorant to life, which was that, you know,

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:14.580
back in 2011 there were women out on nighttime
operations alongside Army Rangers, Navy SEALs,

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:18.889
and other Special Operations teams, you know,
seeing the kind of combat experienced by less

00:06:18.889 --> 00:06:24.430
than 5 percent of the entire United States
military, all while the combat ban was very

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:25.570
much in place.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:30.540
And for me, this story was never simply a
war story; it's really a friendship story,

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:34.710
because so many times we forget that what
we haven't seen is the connection among women

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:39.110
in the same way we've seen the connection
among men and that Kim has covered, you know,

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:45.949
beautifully for years, which is this bond
of war, which we often associate only with

00:06:45.949 --> 00:06:48.580
men, actually has been experienced by women.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.680
And at the end of the day, it's really about
service and sacrifice and patriotism and serving

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:58.340
a cause greater than yourself, and gender
is secondary to it.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:03.250
And it's really now that our stories are catching
up that we're starting to see the reality

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.250
of that.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:11.229
And I was at a(n) event for "Ashley's War"
paperback launch last Wednesday at the National

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:14.950
Infantry Museum in Columbus, Georgia, which
some of you might know, right next to Fort

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:16.100
Benning.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:21.680
And Colonel Fivecoat, who opened Army Ranger
School to women, talked about the charade

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.120
to which we had all been accustomed of the
ground combat rule.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.560
And I thought it was just very powerful because
the truth is the commanders in the field were

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:34.479
working around these rules, and, at war, trying
to figure out how to get the best people in

00:07:34.479 --> 00:07:35.840
the jobs they needed.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.280
And working around systems-you know, one-(chuckles)-one
of the soldiers who's here, one of the young

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:45.150
women from "Ashley's War," was in a job that
was coded for men for years before the combat

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:48.680
ban was lifted-like, officially, a female
could not be in that role when you tried to

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.090
put it in the system.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.650
But her commander wanted the best person for
the job.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:58.310
And so I think really, for me, the best storytelling
simply takes us into a world we didn't know

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:02.390
that already existed, and "Ashley's War" for
me was just a way to tell a story about the

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:07.750
fact that there was an exceptional group of
soldiers who answered when their country asked

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:10.720
well before they officially were there.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.050
DOZIER: Allowed to do it.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.050
LEMMON: Correct.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.800
DOZIER: And of course, if you were an MP and-
LEMMON: Absolutely.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:22.259
DOZIER: -escorting a convoy from one-point
A to point B in Iraq or Afghanistan, you were

00:08:22.259 --> 00:08:25.490
frequently under fire and having to fire back,
so.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:26.490
LEMMON: Absolutely.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.950
And also, the MPs have long been integrated,
which is something I think doesn't come-military

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:35.060
police, for folks who aren't super-familiar
with this conversation, right?-have long been

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:39.620
men and women, and women have been leading
in that arena.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:44.990
And so I think that sometimes we talk about
these issues as if, you know, we just discovered

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:50.982
them yesterday night at 11 p.m. when the truth
is that many of these conversations have been

00:08:50.982 --> 00:08:55.480
going on for years, and women have been very
much a part of America's post-9/11 wars.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:02.910
DOZIER: Agnes, you did some of the studies
on this, including studies of certain organizations

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:08.779
like the Marine Corps that didn't want this
integration to happen-were on record saying

00:09:08.779 --> 00:09:14.149
we want to exclude certain jobs to women.

00:09:14.149 --> 00:09:17.470
So what were some of the drawbacks that people
brought up?

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:25.779
SCHAEFER: Well, so RAND did a large suite
of work on this, and we did work for many

00:09:25.779 --> 00:09:32.569
of the services as well as some work for Juliet
on the standards piece.

00:09:32.569 --> 00:09:40.670
And we didn't, you know, look so much at drawbacks,
per se, but we were really focused on implementation

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:43.709
in much of our work.

00:09:43.709 --> 00:09:46.920
And as a result of that, we sort of focused
on lessons learned.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:53.230
So this is not new, and we have had these
previous waves of integration, of not just

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:58.529
women but other out-groups such as gays and
lesbians.

00:09:58.529 --> 00:10:04.079
And there are some similarities between those
out-groups and those previous waves.

00:10:04.079 --> 00:10:09.670
So we tried to draw out some of those lessons
learned, especially from previous occupations

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:14.610
that were opened, such as engineers, aviation.

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:20.989
And what we found is that we really didn't
do a very good job at documenting that process

00:10:20.989 --> 00:10:23.620
and identifying those lessons learned.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:28.679
So we really emphasized to the services that,
as they do this, they really need to focus

00:10:28.679 --> 00:10:36.050
on monitoring this implementation along the
way so that they can identify issues quickly

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:41.149
and adjust course, and that they can learn
from the process.

00:10:41.149 --> 00:10:47.439
So the process needs to be flexible enough
for them to be able to adjust along the way.

00:10:47.439 --> 00:10:51.929
We also looked at lessons from foreign militaries.

00:10:51.929 --> 00:10:57.990
And one of the major things, especially with
the Marine Corps work, we really tried to

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:02.829
emphasize-initially they came back to us and
said, well, we have this goal.

00:11:02.829 --> 00:11:06.739
If we're going to do this, we have this goal
of very large numbers.

00:11:06.739 --> 00:11:12.329
And we empathized to them that nowhere in
the world are we seeing large numbers.

00:11:12.329 --> 00:11:14.980
We're talking-
DOZIER: So nowhere in the world that has done

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.839
integration are you seeing large numbers of
women in a combat role.

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:21.029
SCHAEFER: That has done this, right.

00:11:21.029 --> 00:11:22.029
Yes.

00:11:22.029 --> 00:11:23.029
So single-low single percentages.

00:11:23.029 --> 00:11:24.860
Those are the types of numbers we're talking
about.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:29.870
And in the special operations community, that's
even smaller.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:37.319
And so we emphasized to them that, you know,
if you're trying to define success in this

00:11:37.319 --> 00:11:41.989
integration process, and you're defining that
based on numbers, you're setting yourself

00:11:41.989 --> 00:11:48.139
up to fail, because the likelihood is so small
that you're going to be able to, you know,

00:11:48.139 --> 00:11:49.829
recruit these large numbers.

00:11:49.829 --> 00:11:54.179
So and that's-you know, we dug into that a
little bit more.

00:11:54.179 --> 00:12:00.060
And that's-there were two main reasons the
numbers were so low in foreign militaries.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.569
This may not be the case here.

00:12:02.569 --> 00:12:03.660
It may be different.

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:10.019
But in foreign militaries it was because women
weren't really interested in these positions,

00:12:10.019 --> 00:12:12.069
and secondly they couldn't make the standards.

00:12:12.069 --> 00:12:14.230
So they were twofold there.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:21.910
DOZIER: So that brings us back to you and
the question of, as the policymaker, do you

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:28.029
have a quota of women that you want to try
to absorb into the combat roles?

00:12:28.029 --> 00:12:33.339
And how do you keep people from-the mantra
I keeping hearing over and over, I'm conscious

00:12:33.339 --> 00:12:37.220
of the fact that there are four women up here
discussing this, so I've got play devil's

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:44.639
advocate-I keep hearing from male officers:
You just know that there's going to be pressure

00:12:44.639 --> 00:12:49.269
on the bureaucracy to lower the standards
to make the numbers.

00:12:49.269 --> 00:12:50.269
BEYLER: Right.

00:12:50.269 --> 00:12:51.550
And so we heard that also.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:56.019
So the short answer to the question is no,
there are no quotas and there are no goals.

00:12:56.019 --> 00:12:57.759
I want to come back to it.

00:12:57.759 --> 00:13:00.040
I do want to make a point, though, about the
Marine Corps.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:04.920
I think what gets lost in this a lot is that
there's this narrative that the Marine Corps

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.170
was opposed to integration.

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:07.639
And I don't think that's true.

00:13:07.639 --> 00:13:12.910
As somebody, again, who's-as a retired Marine
myself, and who has lived this, and have watched

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:18.269
the Marine Corps, the Marine Corps did a significant
good-faith amount of work here.

00:13:18.269 --> 00:13:21.129
And I think it's important to kind of recognize
it.

00:13:21.129 --> 00:13:23.959
They asked for exception, but a very discrete
exception.

00:13:23.959 --> 00:13:28.100
So the Marine Corps actually recommended opening
armor, opening artillery.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:31.160
The Marine Corps recommended opening the vast
majority of their positions.

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:36.160
And they requested from the secretary kind
of a very discrete exception in infantry and

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:37.160
long-range reconnaissance.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:39.389
So I think, again, that's really important.

00:13:39.389 --> 00:13:42.790
And the concerns that the Marine Corps raised,
again, all of the services-the Air Force,

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:45.550
the Navy, SOCOM-had very similar.

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:48.779
So the Marine Corps was not that far off from
everyone else.

00:13:48.779 --> 00:13:51.350
They were just the only ones that chose to
request an exception.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:54.079
So I think that's an important point that
gets lost.

00:13:54.079 --> 00:13:58.459
But with regard to the standards, yes, we
hear that all of the time.

00:13:58.459 --> 00:14:02.850
And so that's why when you look at Secretary
Carter's memo and he has his guiding principles,

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:06.649
he specifically talks about, one, right, the
need to make sure that we have the right standards,

00:14:06.649 --> 00:14:10.129
that they're occupationally specific, that
they're current and they're operationally

00:14:10.129 --> 00:14:14.040
relevant because, again, that's the core,
the standard of everything that we do.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:18.330
And then once we-that was why it was so important
to review and validate the standards, because

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:23.529
now we have an ability to kind of definitively
stand behind a standard that's unemotional,

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:28.440
that we can explain and articulate, and it
is what's required to do the job.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.459
Recognition also, right, that the number of
women that are going to want to do these jobs

00:14:31.459 --> 00:14:35.799
is small, and then the number of women that
can meet the standard beyond that is even

00:14:35.799 --> 00:14:36.799
smaller.

00:14:36.799 --> 00:14:40.422
So there's a full recognition that the numbers
may be very, very small, or not at all.

00:14:40.422 --> 00:14:42.009
And that's what the secretary said.

00:14:42.009 --> 00:14:43.949
So equal opportunity doesn't mean equal participation.

00:14:43.949 --> 00:14:49.009
We recognize there may be very-you know, very
small or there may be none.

00:14:49.009 --> 00:14:50.620
So how do you guard against that, right?

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:56.189
By having a solid standard that everyone-
DOZIER: And are you publishing that standard,

00:14:56.189 --> 00:14:59.089
so that everyone knows what it is and can
tell if it's changes?

00:14:59.089 --> 00:15:00.089
BEYLER: Oh, yeah, absolutely.

00:15:00.089 --> 00:15:02.239
So all of the services-well, let me back up.

00:15:02.239 --> 00:15:07.779
So all of the services have-this is, again-I
don't want to-the services have had standards.

00:15:07.779 --> 00:15:14.220
But we've never drilled down in the manner
that we had this time over the past three

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:15.220
and a half years.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.709
So each of the services, again, went through
every single occupational standard.

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:23.980
And they clearly defined what the standard
was for entry into the occupation, and then

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:28.810
also what the standards are for not only an
entry-level soldier, but also then a sergeant.

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.139
And then the standards for a sergeant first
class or a gunnery sergeant are different

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:33.679
than the standards for a PFC.

00:15:33.679 --> 00:15:39.189
So they have laid out very clearly in their
manuals what are the standards for accession-we

00:15:39.189 --> 00:15:41.660
call them accession-and then the standards
for retention.

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:43.319
So those are out there.

00:15:43.319 --> 00:15:46.989
I would also say that they have institutionalized
the process that we went through over the

00:15:46.989 --> 00:15:52.230
three and a half years, because they learned
a tremendous amount on how to do this right.

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.489
For instance, right, the direct ground combat
role said you could close an occupation of

00:15:55.489 --> 00:15:57.579
women if the vast majority of women couldn't
do it.

00:15:57.579 --> 00:15:58.899
So what did that mean, right?

00:15:58.899 --> 00:16:00.179
That was very subjective.

00:16:00.179 --> 00:16:01.272
And so now it's a definable standard.

00:16:01.272 --> 00:16:02.272
Does that make sense?

00:16:02.272 --> 00:16:03.272
DOZIER: It does.

00:16:03.272 --> 00:16:04.272
It does.

00:16:04.272 --> 00:16:05.759
BEYLER: OK.

00:16:05.759 --> 00:16:09.250
DOZIER: And of course, the critics out there
are going to say, all right, well, let's see

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:10.470
it in operation.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:11.799
LEMMON: Yeah, and just one to that point.

00:16:11.799 --> 00:16:14.709
It was really fascinating, I covered the opening
of the Ranger School to women.

00:16:14.709 --> 00:16:21.589
And I was in pre-ranger in March, I guess,
and then in Florida and swamp phase in August.

00:16:21.589 --> 00:16:26.279
And you know, I went to write one piece, which
was just sort of a straight, you know, Army.

00:16:26.279 --> 00:16:31.819
And I ended up writing a piece that had standards
in it probably 79 times, because it was the

00:16:31.819 --> 00:16:38.019
only word anybody wanted to talk to me about,
whether a woman or a man.

00:16:38.019 --> 00:16:42.279
Our advisors, the women who were serving as
Ranger School advisors, would come up to me

00:16:42.279 --> 00:16:44.220
and say: We never want the standard lowered.

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.309
Make sure your piece reflects that.

00:16:46.309 --> 00:16:50.769
And all of them I would talk to, some of whom
I had known from the process of reporting

00:16:50.769 --> 00:16:54.989
"Ashley's War," would say: I don't care, but
no standard can be lowered.

00:16:54.989 --> 00:16:56.869
And there were lots of questions around that.

00:16:56.869 --> 00:17:00.639
I mean, I think that's why they brought reporters
in a couple different times, trying to show,

00:17:00.639 --> 00:17:02.879
look, there is not going to be a different
standard.

00:17:02.879 --> 00:17:07.370
And at the end of the day, any time humans
are involved there is a level of subjectivity,

00:17:07.370 --> 00:17:11.790
but I think they worked very hard from Fort
Benning leadership to show that this is a

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:16.250
transparent process and the standard is the
thing that's most important.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:21.250
And the one bit of humor is that at 4:45 a.m.-which
I know you know those mornings-when I met

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:28.000
some of the Ranger School leadership at the
Benning gate, this one very storied retired

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.910
ranger said to me: You know, what's amazing
is I never heard people show this much love

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:33.580
for the standard when I was active duty.

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:34.580
(Laughter.)

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:38.821
You know, so I think it was interesting to
hear his perspective that, you know, the standard

00:17:38.821 --> 00:17:40.290
has always been something that's shifting.

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:44.700
But I think now when everything else is shifting
around it, it's even more important for that

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:50.820
standard to be something that everyone understands
and that is not changed for anyone.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:55.790
BEYLER: Well, I do want to-to your point about-standards
will shift, right?

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:57.910
So equipment will change.

00:17:57.910 --> 00:17:59.100
Requirements will change.

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.380
And so standards will change, but it has to
be-

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:04.820
DOZIER: So standards will change because the
equipment changes and therefore the ability

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:06.610
needed to operate that equipment will change?

00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:07.610
BEYLER: Exactly.

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:10.460
But we have clearly defined what it takes
to be successful on the battlefield.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:12.250
And the standard is derived from that.

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.630
And it's derived from what's required today.

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:16.140
So we do recognize there's going to be pressure.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:19.720
There will people that will ask questions
about why not enough.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:25.360
DOZIER: But you'll always have to be able
to drag a comrade who weight 200-plus, plus

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:29.780
their 80-pound pack and their gun, wounded,
out of the line of fire.

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:32.250
Isn't that one of those standards that just
never stops?

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:33.250
BEYLER: Absolutely.

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:35.740
We often use the example of tankers, right?

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:38.140
The round weighs what it weighs.

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:42.020
And you have to take it out of the rack, you
must have the upper body strength to turn

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:44.990
in the seat and load the round into the breach.

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:46.742
It's a defined weight, it's a defined height,
an it's a defined distance.

00:18:46.742 --> 00:18:52.900
And it doesn't matter-I often use the term
man, woman, giraffe, bunny rabbit-that is

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:54.630
what it takes to do that.

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:57.600
And so-
DOZIER: So what about the fact that some studies

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:02.820
have revealed that women do have a higher
incidence of injuries after some of these

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:06.390
heavy weight-bearing occupations?

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:11.800
So you might have a high dropout rate from
these combat positions, where they might wash

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:14.180
out after a year or two.

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:19.850
Will the military find a way to absorb them
back into another role?

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:23.010
SCHAEFER: Yeah, I think that they're working
through that.

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:28.890
But what we found from foreign militaries
is that there are ways to mitigate against

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:30.730
those injury rates.

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:31.730
DOZIER: Like what?

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.050
SCHAEFER: Equipment is one of those.

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:41.540
And I think there-just as they were drilling
down to the standards, which I think really-this

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:45.780
issue of really nailing down those standards
is one of the real benefits that came out

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:51.060
of this whole process, both for men and women,
because they really thought through rationally

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:56.850
what does somebody, regardless of their gender,
need to do for that occupation?

00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:01.220
But they're now drilling down into what kind
of equipment changes they can make, again,

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:03.650
both for men and women.

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:06.940
So for instance, carrying your pack.

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:12.420
You know, you can adjust the waist belt for
people who have shorter torsos, men or women,

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:14.430
you know, those kinds of issues.

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:22.560
I know they're working through, you know,
the armored plates and things like that, again,

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.150
for people with shorter torsos.

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:26.470
So I think they're working through those.

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:30.570
DOZIER: So rather than making it gender specific,
they're just thinking about integrating smaller

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:31.570
people.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:32.570
SCHAEFER: Right.

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:37.770
DOZIER: But there are women-specific injuries
that I've heard about, like the hip displacement

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:42.270
from long marches because the hips are shaped
differently in men and women.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:43.270
SCHAEFER: Right.

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:46.220
So again, four militaries have gone through
this process.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:49.620
Some of them have integrated 20, 25-
DOZIER: Like which ones?

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:50.620
Since you said you looked at four?

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:54.240
SCHAEFER: So Canada has been integrated for
a long time.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:21:01.070
We looked at 55 countries initially and then
narrowed down to our allies.

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:10.450
But so, you know, you can train women over
longer distances and longer times.

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:16.720
So you can sort of graduate their training
so that they're not-they're training over

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:20.910
a longer period of time and they're conditioning
their bodies over a longer period of time.

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:25.580
And that allows them to strengthen-
DOZIER: So the bones have time-I've heard

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:29.570
something about this-that you slowly increase
the weight that the bones are bearing so they

00:21:29.570 --> 00:21:34.810
get thicker instead of stressing them early
and causing them to fracture.

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:35.810
SCHAEFER: Exactly.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:39.030
And they build their core and their upper
body.

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:44.150
So-
DOZIER: But that is slightly changing training

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:45.150
and standards.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:46.150
SCHAEFER: It is.

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:47.150
It is.

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:48.150
So there is a tradeoff there.

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:53.900
You know, they-four militaries have kind of
had them train before they enlist, or before

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:56.460
they start some of these combat occupations.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:59.350
So you could do that training ahead of time.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:03.380
DOZIER: And so that brings us back to the
question that I heard brought up, for instance,

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:11.520
for SEALs and BUD/S, you've got to now add
in a women's room, women's quarters, et cetera.

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:16.440
There's an added cost associated with equality.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:19.600
There's an added cost associated with this
extra training that you're talking about,

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:21.690
and these equipment changes.

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:23.560
Is that worth it to the taxpayer?

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:28.750
BEYLER: And so, first, I think that there
is a lot of discussion that there's added

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:31.930
cost, but when we actually had-when all of
the services actually looked at that, what

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:34.590
they found was there really wasn't.

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:39.240
Most service members live in rooms right now,
they're barracks, they're not open squad base.

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:43.530
So actually, there was very little on the
facilities side of the house that had to be

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:46.030
done-
DOZIER: So they're not in a room full of a

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:48.320
bunch of bunks, like we've seen in the old
movies.

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:49.320
BEYLER: Exactly.

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:53.620
So the only-for instance at BUD/S, at the
SEAL training school out in California, they

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:56.070
just had to build one new restroom.

00:22:56.070 --> 00:23:00.590
So that was the extent of the facilities modifications
that needed to be done.

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:03.530
And they wanted-they needed to do some stuff
to the barracks, but the costs there were

00:23:03.530 --> 00:23:04.530
very minor.

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:08.170
And the same thing, both the Marine Corps,
the Army, and the Air Force all came back

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:12.820
and said they didn't need to do any facilities
modernization, that they were good where they

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:13.820
were.

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:14.820
DOZIER: OK.

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:17.540
BEYLER: So on the training side of the house
though, I would say that-so especially on

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:21.450
the special operations side, they have always
had these pre-accession courses.

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:24.250
So these courses have always been there.

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:26.530
So nobody is developing any new courses.

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:29.860
So with regard to, was there a cost to develop
something, no, actually, not.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:33.340
And so the benefit, though, is huge for both
men and women.

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:37.750
We learned better ways to prepare people to
succeed at the school.

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:41.520
Again, so again better ways to make sure,
again, we're strengthening bone density and

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:46.500
bone mass, and making sure-teaching them ways
to do things so that they don't injure themselves,

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:48.340
use the core, the things that Agnes said.

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:52.390
So again, all of those benefits go to both
men and women.

00:23:52.390 --> 00:23:59.250
DOZIER: So in the few minutes before I open
it up to the members, I wanted to discuss

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:03.100
some of the emotional questions that get brought
up.

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:06.840
Now, you're a former serving Marine.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:13.690
You mentioned as we were chatting before this
that there was a little bit of trepidation

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:18.690
when you took a commanding role at one point,
among some of the people working for you.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:19.770
BEYLER: Yes.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:23.120
So I was privileged, I guess, to get commissioned
after the Gulf War.

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:26.610
So I was in that first year group of female
combat engineers when the Marine Corps opened

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:27.610
that to women.

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:32.890
So, of course, I experienced firsthand what
it was like to integrate into an MOS that

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:34.380
had been completely closed.

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:36.230
And what I found was, same thing, right.

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:42.390
So there was a lot of emotion, a lot of concern,
a lot of people calling my Marines to express

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:46.190
consternation about the fact that they were
being now led by a woman, and what the concern

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:47.190
was.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:50.880
But I think, like, my own personal experience
was there were a lot of myths, there was a

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:52.120
lot of confusion.

00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:56.800
But once they understood who I was and what
I did, and that I did exactly what they did,

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:57.800
everything was fine.

00:24:57.800 --> 00:25:00.040
So we see that in the special operations community.

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:01.430
We see that across the board.

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:05.750
When we first started talking about this in
2010, we saw a lot of those same concerns

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:11.030
across the Army, and these misunderstandings
of what a woman could or could not-

00:25:11.030 --> 00:25:12.110
DOZIER: Misunderstandings or misconceptions?

00:25:12.110 --> 00:25:13.110
BEYLER: Misconceptions, actually.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:14.110
Yes, thank you.

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:15.110
That's better.

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:16.110
Of what they could or couldn't do.

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:20.550
And I think-again, I felt-once you get there
and they get-they understand, and they see

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:23.490
you, and they see you operate those things
over time go away.

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:32.350
DOZIER: So how about the other two sort of
lightning rod questions, how men respond when

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:38.540
a woman is under fire and what happens to
esprit de corps when you have women in the

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:41.520
mix and all of a sudden you've got guys flirting
with women, et cetera?

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:46.900
These are two things that are always brought
up after the first beer when you get a group

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:48.830
of special operators together.

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:52.710
LEMMON: Well, maybe I'll just make a couple
points on this.

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:57.180
First one is, and as this came up in the interview
with General McChrystal when we were working

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.560
on "Ashley's War" is that women have been
in Delta for a long time.

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:03.260
Right, so a lot of this-
DOZIER: In Delta Force.

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:04.260
LEMMON: Right.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:05.260
And so, you know-
BEYLER: Clandestine.

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:06.260
LEMMON: Right.

00:26:06.260 --> 00:26:07.260
And quietly, and obviously.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:08.260
But you know, he brought that up.

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:13.850
Is like, you know, this is not a terribly
new conversation.

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:17.700
But the second thing that I think is really
important is that oftentimes I don't think

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:21.500
we give enough credit to men alongside whom
these women are serving.

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:26.840
And it was something I saw in two years of
working on-you know, trying to talk, and talk,

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:31.320
and talk to people who had been on the front
lines in the special operations community,

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:36.930
some Rangers who had done 12, 13, 14 deployments
in the post-9/11 wars, with a country that

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:39.830
barely knew people were doing one, right?

00:26:39.830 --> 00:26:41.360
And you would talk to them.

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:45.750
And what they would tell you was: I want somebody
next to me who is competent, who is skilled,

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:47.450
and who will make sure that they can do the
job.

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:53.290
And if you, you know, pay your rent out there
night, you earn your seat on the bird, period.

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:59.360
And I think that, you know, the demands of
combat, the very life and death stakes of

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:04.510
these wars, erases so much of this discussion
that goes on in nice rooms in, you know, cities

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:09.300
where power goes on when you flip a switch
and the roads are smooth and there's infrastructure

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:10.470
that works, right?

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:16.020
When you're in a very tough parts of the world
when there is a very real war going on, what

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:20.830
these guys were focused on was can you do
your job, will you slow me down, and do you

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:23.270
make a difference out there every single night
and find what we need?

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:28.300
You know, one of the MPs who was in-military
police-who's in "Ashley's War" was telling

00:27:28.300 --> 00:27:32.890
me the story about the SEAL team she was working
with, that actually wasn't terribly excited

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.350
to get her when she first showed up.

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:39.920
But you know, one of the first night out she
found the intel item they were looking for

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:44.480
in a baby's wet diaper in the quarters that
would never have searched, which mean that

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:48.840
they found the person, they got the thing,
and everybody got home safely without having

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:50.640
to be out there any longer.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:53.420
And for the SEALs, you know, then they train-even
when there were regulations about when there

00:27:53.420 --> 00:27:57.270
were regulations about whether women could
fast rope or not, they actually trained their

00:27:57.270 --> 00:28:00.520
CSD to fast rope because they were like, look,
if you're going to be on mission with us,

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:02.400
then you need to know everything we do.

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:05.010
And we decide if you're ready.

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:09.890
And so I think what you see is people who've
seen a lot of war, which is a huge percent

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:14.880
of a 1 percent, are much less focused on these
kinds of discussions, and much more focused

00:28:14.880 --> 00:28:17.730
on whether you can deliver on the battlefield.

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:20.110
And I don't think they get enough credit for
that.

00:28:20.110 --> 00:28:25.090
DOZIER: And, Agnes, you were-in studying the
different militaries, we have to bring up

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:30.750
the Israeli example, they took women out of
their combat units because the men fell apart

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:32.320
when they saw the women injured.

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:34.059
SCHAEFER: Yeah.

00:28:34.059 --> 00:28:41.670
I mean, there-a lot of people put up the Israeli
example as kind of the poster child.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:48.130
But they have a lot of constraints on their
women as well, in terms of rules of engagement

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:50.240
and things like that.

00:28:50.240 --> 00:28:53.240
And they couldn't be on the frontlines and
those kinds of things.

00:28:53.240 --> 00:29:01.760
So you can't-it's not directly analogous to
what we're talking about here in the U.S.

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:08.751
But I mean, we definitely covered and studied
this issue of cohesion, because that was a

00:29:08.751 --> 00:29:15.080
major concern across the services, because
they were concerned that if cohesion deteriorated

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:18.520
that would impact mission effectiveness.

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:23.650
And what we found is that really, you know,
during these previous waves of integration

00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:29.340
of women into these MOSs, there was no degradation
of cohesion.

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:35.610
And the reason for that is that really it
was this focus of task cohesion.

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:40.510
People were concerned about whether you could
do the job or not, regardless of whether or

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:44.010
not the person to your right or left is a
man or a woman.

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:48.110
And so, you know, that task cohesion was really
the center.

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:53.530
People don't necessarily need to like each
other to work together, but they really care

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:56.650
about whether the person can do the job.

00:29:56.650 --> 00:29:59.350
And this gets back to the standards piece,
I think.

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:01.600
So this is why standards are so important.

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:07.680
DOZIER: So the survey-the SOCOM survey that
you did, when you asked, you know, what do

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:13.780
you feel about women joining combat units,
and there was a highly negative response,

00:30:13.780 --> 00:30:19.570
was there a way to differentiate between who
had served alongside women and who hadn't?

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:21.760
SCHAEFER: Yes, we found that.

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:28.390
And this is actually very similar to the work
that we did when we were looking at, you know,

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:30.490
the repeal of "don't ask, don't tell."

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:36.780
We looked at cohesion issues and we did a
large survey then, which at that point it

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:41.550
was-you know, they couldn't openly admit that
they were gay or lesbian, but we were able

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:44.210
to find a way to survey them.

00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:50.280
And we found that, you know, when we talked
to service members, those that had served

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:58.580
along with women, in higher headquarters in
particular, and in the case of "don't ask,

00:30:58.580 --> 00:31:03.110
don't tell," those who had interacted with
gays or lesbians, were much more amenable

00:31:03.110 --> 00:31:07.420
to the fact that this would be OK.

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:12.510
And so this is-this is very typical of when
you, you know, integrate these outgroups.

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:17.370
If you've had contact with them, you tend
to be more-the survey data indicates that

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:19.630
you tend to be more amendable to that.

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:20.720
DOZIER: OK.

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:22.180
Well, it's 9:00.

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:27.010
So at this time, I'd like to invite members
to join our conversation with their questions.

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:30.420
A reminder that this meeting is on the record.

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:36.150
And also, please speak into the microphone
that are in front of you, and state your name

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:37.880
and affiliation.

00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:42.740
Please limit yourself to one question and
keep it concise to allow as many members as

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:46.450
possible to speak.

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:52.150
And while people think of their questions,
I will ask one more.

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:53.640
Selective service.

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:59.420
Now that combat roles are open, is it time
for every young woman to go into the Post

00:31:59.420 --> 00:32:01.910
Office and sign up for a potential draft?

00:32:01.910 --> 00:32:06.920
BEYLER: So what Secretary Carter said is that
the issue of selective service has got to

00:32:06.920 --> 00:32:09.880
be part of a broader national discussion that's
not just DOD.

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:12.710
So we're-we think it's time to have that conversation.

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:14.630
We're prepared to do that.

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:18.320
But that's-
DOZIER: OK, well, saying let's have a conversation

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:22.070
is not stating which way you think the conversation
should go.

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:23.420
That's very careful.

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:24.770
BEYLER: Oh, absolutely.

00:32:24.770 --> 00:32:30.360
And the secretary's been very clear to say
this is outside the purview of just the Department

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:32.540
of Defense.

00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:34.380
And so we're not there yet.

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:38.720
That's something we absolutely need to look
at, but again it's beyond just this very discrete

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:39.720
issue.

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:44.140
So we need to make sure that we take everything
into consideration before that decision is

00:32:44.140 --> 00:32:45.140
made.

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:49.960
DOZIER: So, let's see, I wanted to ask members
to tilt their placards up when they have a

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:51.720
question.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:53.200
I haven't done one of-ma'am.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:59.470
Q: I don't know if the mics are-
DOZIER: Can you lean into that mic?

00:32:59.470 --> 00:33:00.470
Thank you.

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:01.470
Q: Sure.

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:02.470
Sure.

00:33:02.470 --> 00:33:03.470
So I'm Jen Leonard.

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:04.470
I'm with International Crisis Group.

00:33:04.470 --> 00:33:05.470
Thank you so much for your comments.

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:10.540
It's not an issue I dug really deep into,
but tracking in headlines.

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:13.580
We've talked a lot about standards, everyone
meeting standards.

00:33:13.580 --> 00:33:21.580
I'd be great to hear from you whether anecdotally
or research, evidence driven, about exceeding

00:33:21.580 --> 00:33:27.820
those standards and unique qualifications,
and whether and how in your research, in terms

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:34.380
of looking at allies, et cetera, there have
been bit of evidence that have shared with

00:33:34.380 --> 00:33:44.020
you why women might excel at a particular
category, set of issues, resilience, troubleshooting,

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:45.980
et cetera?

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:49.590
If there's not, where are we going to track
that too?

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:50.660
Thank you.

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:54.080
DOZIER: Who would like to take a stab at that.

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:56.230
BEYLER: Or I can.

00:33:56.230 --> 00:34:01.880
So, yes, a lot of people talk about a concern
about don't lower standards, but you're absolutely

00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:02.880
right.

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:06.430
So we identified places across the board where
perhaps the standard was too low and needed

00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:07.880
to be raised.

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:09.470
We use often the airborne example.

00:34:09.470 --> 00:34:14.700
The pack weight had been historically 45 pounds,
and that had been around since World War II.

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:17.379
That's not reflective of what you need to
carry on today's battlefield.

00:34:17.379 --> 00:34:21.379
So that was an example of a physical standard
that actually needed to be raised in this

00:34:21.379 --> 00:34:22.379
particular case.

00:34:22.379 --> 00:34:26.561
Now, with regard to the things that you were
saying, kind of more on the cognitive, resilience

00:34:26.561 --> 00:34:30.889
side of the house, so for instance in the
special operations community there are-they

00:34:30.889 --> 00:34:37.559
do test and assess people for decision making
skills, emotional stability, all of that type

00:34:37.559 --> 00:34:38.559
of stuff.

00:34:38.559 --> 00:34:42.840
But those are already standards that have
been in place, so again, right, but there

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:44.970
are certainly areas where women will excel.

00:34:44.970 --> 00:34:47.609
And we'll see that, hopefully, come out as
we move forward.

00:34:47.609 --> 00:34:49.929
I don't know if you want to add anything to
that.

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:53.149
SCHAEFER: Yeah, so the data on this is really
kind of mixed.

00:34:53.149 --> 00:34:58.130
And this is why we emphasized to the services
that as they do this they really need to monitor

00:34:58.130 --> 00:35:02.920
where there's progress and see where they're
seeing those areas.

00:35:02.920 --> 00:35:09.819
And you know, in deficiencies they may be
able to, you know, do things to help them.

00:35:09.819 --> 00:35:14.890
So yeah, unfortunately it's very mixed.

00:35:14.890 --> 00:35:18.080
DOZIER: OK.

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:19.670
Sunil?

00:35:19.670 --> 00:35:22.849
Oh, Laura.

00:35:22.849 --> 00:35:27.640
Q: Thank you.

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:28.980
Laura Liswood.

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:34.310
Three years on DACOWITS, so some sense of
this, and 13 years a reserve sergeant Metropolitan

00:35:34.310 --> 00:35:35.310
Police Department.

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.239
DOZIER: And can you remind people what DACOWITS
is?

00:35:37.239 --> 00:35:40.420
Q: Oh, yes, the Defense Advisory Committee
on Women in the Services.

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:45.319
It's been around since the Korean War, and
has looked at various elements.

00:35:45.319 --> 00:35:49.499
One of the elements that we looked at was
women on submarines.

00:35:49.499 --> 00:35:54.640
And some of the out-group objections to blacks
on submarines, you could have just taken the

00:35:54.640 --> 00:36:00.319
language out of that and said why women shouldn't
be on submarines-unit cohesion, too close,

00:36:00.319 --> 00:36:03.890
wives don't like it, et cetera, et cetera.

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:09.069
My question is, in addition to this issue
of the value added of diversity, which is

00:36:09.069 --> 00:36:14.910
getting more and more play in corporations
and things like that, and therefore tracking

00:36:14.910 --> 00:36:20.010
that, which I think could be incredibly interesting
to see different perspectives, do you see

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:29.130
that there's going to be any diminution, increase,
around issues of sexual harassment and issues

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:30.720
of that nature?

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:37.319
DOZIER: Because these women are in units that
have never had women before, or something?

00:36:37.319 --> 00:36:38.319
Q: Yes.

00:36:38.319 --> 00:36:43.490
And so is it more of, oh, now we respect them
and we understand what value that they have,

00:36:43.490 --> 00:36:48.489
et cetera, versus, well, we have these close
quarters and we have these issues?

00:36:48.489 --> 00:36:51.460
BEYLER: Oh, sure.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:53.950
So I would say do we expect an increase?

00:36:53.950 --> 00:36:58.010
I certainly hope not, but I think we recognize
that that's something we need to look at.

00:36:58.010 --> 00:37:02.549
That's what each of the services-to the point
that we were talking about surveys before-that's

00:37:02.549 --> 00:37:08.039
why all of the service-you hear a lot of discussion
about the SOCOM survey-but all of the services

00:37:08.039 --> 00:37:10.369
did surveys of one sort, right?

00:37:10.369 --> 00:37:11.369
Why?

00:37:11.369 --> 00:37:14.569
Because we need to identify where there are
those misconceptions.

00:37:14.569 --> 00:37:20.200
And then we need to development training or
we need to make sure that explain where those

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:25.309
things are incorrect, so we don't have that,
and as well to identify any potential issues

00:37:25.309 --> 00:37:26.309
that need to be addressed.

00:37:26.309 --> 00:37:30.569
But we don't view this issue as anything-you
know, sexual harassment and sexual assault,

00:37:30.569 --> 00:37:31.809
it's not tolerated in the department.

00:37:31.809 --> 00:37:32.809
It's not acceptable.

00:37:32.809 --> 00:37:36.510
And so this is no different than any other
effort, and we view it no differently.

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:40.760
DOZIER: I was going to say, Gayle, can you
point out some of your folks in the crowd

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:41.859
who-
LEMMON: Yeah.

00:37:41.859 --> 00:37:46.450
And there are two things I want to just get
out that we were talking about before.

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:51.730
So the program, they have the cultural support
teams, which was a very benign name for a

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:56.819
pretty groundbreaking concept, was created
by Admiral Olson, who was the first Navy SEAL

00:37:56.819 --> 00:37:59.309
to lead Special Operations Command.

00:37:59.309 --> 00:38:03.359
And you know, I had CSPAN caller the other
day say that, you know, is this part of the

00:38:03.359 --> 00:38:04.980
feminist agenda?

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:08.339
And I said, well, I don't think Admiral Olson
was known for his feminist agenda.

00:38:08.339 --> 00:38:13.829
I think he was really focused on the security
gap that his forces were facing in the field.

00:38:13.829 --> 00:38:18.809
And when I had the pleasure of talking with
him in the process of reporting, he was asking

00:38:18.809 --> 00:38:23.329
me about these soldiers, the young women who
are here, who were part of this team.

00:38:23.329 --> 00:38:27.200
And he was asking me what they were like,
and I was filling him in, because he had retired

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:29.369
just before the program had been in full swing.

00:38:29.369 --> 00:38:32.140
And he said, so they're just like the men?

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:33.140
And I said, yeah.

00:38:33.140 --> 00:38:37.470
He said, you know, what I look for in special
operations is physical fit problem solvers.

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:42.119
He said, people want to ascribe all kinds
of superhuman traits to the special operations

00:38:42.119 --> 00:38:45.329
community, but that's at the core of what
we're seeking.

00:38:45.329 --> 00:38:47.279
And I think that was really important.

00:38:47.279 --> 00:38:52.650
And to Laura's point about African Americans
and other integration, it was really interesting

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:57.799
talking to one of the Rangers who did pre-mission
training with the soldiers in "Ashley's War."

00:38:57.799 --> 00:39:02.970
And he was very skeptical when they said he
had to go train girls-that was his official

00:39:02.970 --> 00:39:07.539
assignment from when he was at Benning at
go down to Fort Bragg and-or, to go to Bragg

00:39:07.539 --> 00:39:10.299
to train members of this team.

00:39:10.299 --> 00:39:15.349
And you know, really at the end of eight days,
this guy who by no means would he care about

00:39:15.349 --> 00:39:20.609
any kind of equality or agenda, you know,
that is not his world, you know, he said:

00:39:20.609 --> 00:39:25.070
You know, I looked around and I thought, you
know, these may be one day be our own Tuskegee

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:26.070
Airmen, right?

00:39:26.070 --> 00:39:30.730
Like, these people who are going to make history
and no one knows they exist.

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:32.640
And I do think that there are a lot of parallels.

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:34.099
And it's hard to see that in the moment.

00:39:34.099 --> 00:39:38.739
I think it's hard for all of us who chronicle
it to see it in the much wider picture.

00:39:38.739 --> 00:39:43.789
But I think 50 years from now, this moment
will look very different than it does right

00:39:43.789 --> 00:39:44.789
now.

00:39:44.789 --> 00:39:49.180
DOZIER: Oh, and-
LEMMON: And I wanted to just point out, there

00:39:49.180 --> 00:39:52.960
are three of the soldiers who are in "Ashley's
War" who are here, and maybe you could just

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:55.369
stand up for a quick moment, yeah.

00:39:55.369 --> 00:39:56.369
Q: Hi.

00:39:56.369 --> 00:39:57.780
My name is Amy Sexauer.

00:39:57.780 --> 00:40:00.880
I'm still active duty captain serving at Fort
Bragg.

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:01.920
DOZIER: Oh, sorry.

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:02.920
Microphone, please.

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:03.920
Q: Oh.

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:04.920
Hi.

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:07.310
Captain Amy Sexauer, still active duty Army,
serving at Fort Bragg.

00:40:07.310 --> 00:40:11.230
Q: Captain Rachel Washburn, also serving at
Fort Bragg.

00:40:11.230 --> 00:40:12.239
Q: Captain Meghan Curran.

00:40:12.239 --> 00:40:14.690
I'm in the Massachusetts Army Reserve now.

00:40:14.690 --> 00:40:16.789
DOZIER: And can I put the three of you on
the spot?

00:40:16.789 --> 00:40:24.220
Have any of you encountered sexual harassment
when you were in these unique jobs, when you

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:25.599
first entered them?

00:40:25.599 --> 00:40:31.780
Q: I think, to Gayle's point, we don't give
enough credit to the men in this conversation

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:33.270
a lot of the time.

00:40:33.270 --> 00:40:36.710
I think that what we have to remember, especially
with the groups that we worked with, the special

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:40.950
operations community, they're consummate professionals.

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:46.390
And I think from my personal experience I
never saw anything to that effect.

00:40:46.390 --> 00:40:53.440
And I really just-I think that that conversation
is not-is not addressed as much, that we need

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:57.390
to give more credit to the men in these situations.

00:40:57.390 --> 00:41:01.849
And they are there do a mission and we were
there to enable a mission.

00:41:01.849 --> 00:41:05.540
And it's almost as simple as that.

00:41:05.540 --> 00:41:12.479
And a lot of this other conversation really
doesn't matter when you're in a high stakes

00:41:12.479 --> 00:41:13.479
environment.

00:41:13.479 --> 00:41:14.880
So that's my personal experience.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:17.980
I don't know if-
DOZIER: Thank you very much.

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:19.749
LEMMON: Can I just add one thing?

00:41:19.749 --> 00:41:20.749
It was really fascinating.

00:41:20.749 --> 00:41:24.310
When I was working on-actually, I was just
telling Laura Sullivan this-when I was working

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:27.839
on "Ashley's War," you know, it's Washington,
so you don't talk about what you're working

00:41:27.839 --> 00:41:28.839
on very much.

00:41:28.839 --> 00:41:29.839
And occasionally I would.

00:41:29.839 --> 00:41:31.380
And I'd say it's a special operations story.

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:32.880
And people would be like, oh, that's awesome.

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:35.900
Oh, I love "Lone Survivor."

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:36.900
And I love "American Sniper."

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:39.339
And you'd say, oh, and there are women in
it.

00:41:39.339 --> 00:41:42.319
And it was like crickets.

00:41:42.319 --> 00:41:47.400
And inevitably, the next question from men
and from women, was: Oh, is about rape or

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:49.450
PTSD?

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:53.640
And that was really eye opening for me, the
first, second, third time I heard it.

00:41:53.640 --> 00:41:57.829
And by the end I was kind of prepared, because
it is absolutely urgent that the issue of

00:41:57.829 --> 00:42:01.210
military sexual assault is front and center,
100 percent.

00:42:01.210 --> 00:42:06.660
But when the valor story is missing, it affects
the rest of the conversation about service,

00:42:06.660 --> 00:42:08.690
period, whether it's male or female.

00:42:08.690 --> 00:42:12.489
DOZIER: But I thank Laura for bringing it
up, though, because that's one the reasons

00:42:12.489 --> 00:42:19.589
that DACOWITS was originally founded, was
to represent women in the military writ large.

00:42:19.589 --> 00:42:23.849
So it's a question that needs to be asked,
but it's great to have it just knocked on

00:42:23.849 --> 00:42:26.880
the head, especially by somebody who served
out there.

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:27.880
Sunil.

00:42:27.880 --> 00:42:28.880
Q: Hi.

00:42:28.880 --> 00:42:30.319
My name's Sunil Desai.

00:42:30.319 --> 00:42:35.170
I'm a retired U.S. Marine as well, infantry
officer.

00:42:35.170 --> 00:42:36.900
I love the book.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:41.700
I read the whole book and was intrigued by
it.

00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:42.999
And I appreciate everything that I've heard.

00:42:42.999 --> 00:42:46.440
I agree with pretty much everything.

00:42:46.440 --> 00:42:49.230
And just wanted to make two small points.

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:50.230
Just my perspective.

00:42:50.230 --> 00:42:54.099
You know, I married an Army officer who could
do more push-pullups, pushups too, that most

00:42:54.099 --> 00:42:56.950
of the Marines that I knew.

00:42:56.950 --> 00:43:02.779
And so I respected that, amongst a lot of
other things.

00:43:02.779 --> 00:43:09.710
But the first point would be, regarding the
standards, and I never bought into the idea

00:43:09.710 --> 00:43:16.950
that that was some of the reasons that women
shouldn't serve in combat roles, even when

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:19.279
I was a young officer.

00:43:19.279 --> 00:43:26.250
But one point that didn't come up is, while
there is a pass/fail standard, I mean, you

00:43:26.250 --> 00:43:31.380
have to meet some bare minimum, the standard
still exists on a scale.

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:39.790
And the military, in addition to the requirement
for, you know, tactical execution on the battlefield,

00:43:39.790 --> 00:43:43.460
there's this inspiration that comes from the
people who can achieve even more, right?

00:43:43.460 --> 00:43:46.690
And I think even the women in the book, you
see that from them.

00:43:46.690 --> 00:43:51.160
They really admire people who are super fit,
and even more than necessary.

00:43:51.160 --> 00:43:55.339
And so there's an element of that that should
be added to the conversation.

00:43:55.339 --> 00:44:00.160
I mean, the senior-you've spoke with some
of these special ops leaders who are physical-I

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:05.500
mean, well-beyond, even as they get older
they don't hold themselves to the sliding

00:44:05.500 --> 00:44:07.980
standard that will allow for the aging.

00:44:07.980 --> 00:44:11.730
They hold themselves to the original, the
highest standard.

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:15.200
And that's admired and valued and respected.

00:44:15.200 --> 00:44:18.171
And then the one other-just a small one.

00:44:18.171 --> 00:44:22.770
And you touched on it with your last question
about the draft, and I have a daughter now

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:23.770
too.

00:44:23.770 --> 00:44:30.029
But I think beyond that, this subject, and
the experiences, speak to the even broader

00:44:30.029 --> 00:44:34.280
question of what's happening not only in our
country but in our society writ large, just

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:39.499
in terms of how families are evolving, how
work is evolving.

00:44:39.499 --> 00:44:45.640
And there's several other current books that
are hot in the CFR world, whether "Lean In"

00:44:45.640 --> 00:44:48.150
or "Unfinished Business," right?

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:50.839
And then there's another one called "The End
of May."

00:44:50.839 --> 00:44:53.880
So I think all these dynamics are happening.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:57.259
And they all have to be thought of holistically
together if we're going to get to the right

00:44:57.259 --> 00:44:58.640
answer for everybody.

00:44:58.640 --> 00:45:05.619
DOZIER: So one of the things I hear you saying
is that physical fitness might be one of the

00:45:05.619 --> 00:45:13.759
ways just to put all doubts to rest, if you're
more fit than everyone else.

00:45:13.759 --> 00:45:23.680
But going back to this question of participation
in society and are we uneven-it's the question

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:27.859
that Ash Carter doesn't want to be the only
one asking.

00:45:27.859 --> 00:45:39.270
But do we need women in the draft to follow
through with this-you know, if you're going

00:45:39.270 --> 00:45:44.859
to allow women to be in combat roles in the
military, shouldn't they be part of the wider

00:45:44.859 --> 00:45:49.020
communities-while serving the wider community?

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:54.460
I guess I'm stumbling around on this one,
because it's such-it's such a-like, I can

00:45:54.460 --> 00:46:00.809
see my own parents would have freaked out
about it, but then you look at some of the

00:46:00.809 --> 00:46:03.890
discussions brought up by people like General
Stanley McChrystal, that shouldn't we have

00:46:03.890 --> 00:46:07.259
at least a wider national service?

00:46:07.259 --> 00:46:10.000
So where does that stand, that discussion?

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:13.779
BEYLER: Well, right, again, that's the crux
of the issue, right?

00:46:13.779 --> 00:46:18.060
We need to have a larger national discussion
on, right, public service, national service,

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:19.710
and where do we need to go from there.

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:23.589
I think from our perspective, right, we have
an all-volunteer force, and we're meeting

00:46:23.589 --> 00:46:25.259
the requirements that we have right now.

00:46:25.259 --> 00:46:29.339
Now, whether we have a draft, or don't have
a draft, or whether they expand Selective

00:46:29.339 --> 00:46:34.980
Service, or they don't expand Selective Service,
we think certainly we need to be part of that

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:35.980
discussion.

00:46:35.980 --> 00:46:40.049
But it doesn't-we are-we have what we need,
and we have great people coming in.

00:46:40.049 --> 00:46:43.900
And that was what Secretary Carter was kind
of focused on, on the all-volunteer force.

00:46:43.900 --> 00:46:47.809
He didn't want to restrict his ability to
recruit to only half the population.

00:46:47.809 --> 00:46:53.069
DOZIER: But isn't that, in a sense-well, it's
ducking the question, that if you're going

00:46:53.069 --> 00:46:58.759
to make this a touchstone of your administration
that you fought for this level of equality

00:46:58.759 --> 00:47:05.720
for first having gays in the military and
then allowing women to go into combat roles,

00:47:05.720 --> 00:47:14.220
why not follow it through with, and we believe-if
we're backing these two principles, why not

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:17.839
take a stand on having women in Selective
Service?

00:47:17.839 --> 00:47:21.539
BEYLER: So, right, I understand the question.

00:47:21.539 --> 00:47:26.099
I guess-I guess we're-again, where we are
is we're at-we're at the beginning of that

00:47:26.099 --> 00:47:27.099
discussion.

00:47:27.099 --> 00:47:31.999
And I think it's just-to kind of take a position
on it before we've actually even had the full-blown

00:47:31.999 --> 00:47:34.260
conversation is probably premature.

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:38.530
LEMMON: But one thing I think is so interesting
is that we talk about equality, and I think

00:47:38.530 --> 00:47:41.049
it's also really about talent, right?

00:47:41.049 --> 00:47:45.219
It's about finding the right pool of the right
people for the right jobs.

00:47:45.219 --> 00:47:47.650
And I think that's the national security discussion.

00:47:47.650 --> 00:47:49.670
That's the national security question.

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:51.499
It's not about social programs, right?

00:47:51.499 --> 00:47:55.349
I think it's about security gaps and about
having the best force.

00:47:55.349 --> 00:48:00.079
And I think the draft question is fascinating
because what-the Selective Service was abolished

00:48:00.079 --> 00:48:04.490
in '73, and then brought back in 1980 when
the Russians invaded Afghanistan.

00:48:04.490 --> 00:48:10.170
And, you know, that was a long time ago now,
and think about how much war has been fought

00:48:10.170 --> 00:48:11.170
since then.

00:48:11.170 --> 00:48:13.500
I think whole conversation should be updated.

00:48:13.500 --> 00:48:15.799
You know, should 18-year-old young men have
to register?

00:48:15.799 --> 00:48:17.219
Should 18-year-old young women?

00:48:17.219 --> 00:48:21.289
What are the options for-I mean, you know,
one parent told me, I would much rather have

00:48:21.289 --> 00:48:23.559
my daughter defend my country than my son-(laughter)-you
know?

00:48:23.559 --> 00:48:27.489
I mean, parents come up to you all the time
and tell you sort of very colorful things.

00:48:27.489 --> 00:48:32.170
But, I mean, I think it goes to the fact that
it's a sort of an outdated way of viewing

00:48:32.170 --> 00:48:34.509
a world that has fundamentally shifted.

00:48:34.509 --> 00:48:38.789
Because, to your points, you know, about looking
at things in siloes, this is a much bigger

00:48:38.789 --> 00:48:43.549
conversation about a world that is on fire
in many different places, with a shape of

00:48:43.549 --> 00:48:49.500
a threat that has changed and, I think, an
international architecture that hasn't changed

00:48:49.500 --> 00:48:54.859
with it, and, going to that, a national infrastructure
that hasn't really kept pace with the time.

00:48:54.859 --> 00:48:59.430
So how do you think of the broader question
about how you defend and protect and serve?

00:48:59.430 --> 00:49:03.740
DOZIER: So you're say that the policy was
really changed because it's about getting

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:09.799
access to talent for the mission at hand rather
than an issue of overall fairness and equalizing

00:49:09.799 --> 00:49:10.799
the playing field.

00:49:10.799 --> 00:49:11.799
BEYLER: Oh absolutely.

00:49:11.799 --> 00:49:13.400
Secretary Carter was very clear about that.

00:49:13.400 --> 00:49:15.460
He was about recruit-about the all-volunteer
force.

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:22.769
He said, you never know where your next Olympic
athlete is going to come, so why would I voluntarily

00:49:22.769 --> 00:49:24.200
just cut my recruiting pool in half?

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:25.239
It made no sense to him.

00:49:25.239 --> 00:49:32.880
DOZIER: Agnes, in your study of other militaries,
did you find-were there other militaries that

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:37.869
draw from the whole pool of the population
in terms of a draft?

00:49:37.869 --> 00:49:39.319
SCHAEFER: Yes, yes.

00:49:39.319 --> 00:49:47.299
And, you know, the reason for integrating
these combat roles was different across the

00:49:47.299 --> 00:49:48.319
countries, too.

00:49:48.319 --> 00:49:51.960
Many of them were forced to.

00:49:51.960 --> 00:49:58.769
They had equal opportunity issues, lawsuits,
things like that.

00:49:58.769 --> 00:50:06.539
So it was interesting because-you know, it
was interesting to see those various reasons.

00:50:06.539 --> 00:50:11.170
And some of them thought that, you know, this
was the right thing to do for equality reasons.

00:50:11.170 --> 00:50:12.490
We took a different route.

00:50:12.490 --> 00:50:20.650
And so, you know, their strategies have been
different, too, that kind of shaped the way

00:50:20.650 --> 00:50:24.880
that they actually implemented this.

00:50:24.880 --> 00:50:28.400
And some of them had quotas, which we found
actually didn't work.

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:32.989
They never met them-(chuckles)-because their
numbers were so small.

00:50:32.989 --> 00:50:36.380
And, you know, that sort of set back the process.

00:50:36.380 --> 00:50:38.650
DOZIER: So what were the numbers?

00:50:38.650 --> 00:50:40.609
Like, what can we expect?

00:50:40.609 --> 00:50:44.289
What was an average of numbers of women in
combat roles?

00:50:44.289 --> 00:50:50.599
SCHAEFER: So in no-yeah, in none of the countries
that we looked at did we find anything above

00:50:50.599 --> 00:50:52.450
low single percentages in these-in these roles.

00:50:52.450 --> 00:50:54.589
DOZIER: You're saying 2 percent or 9 percent?

00:50:54.589 --> 00:50:56.450
SCHAEFER: Like 5 (percent).

00:50:56.450 --> 00:50:58.979
Six (percent) was the highest.

00:50:58.979 --> 00:51:00.769
So-
BEYLER: Canada's at-

00:51:00.769 --> 00:51:01.769
DOZIER: Absolutely.

00:51:01.769 --> 00:51:02.769
SCHAEFER: Yeah.

00:51:02.769 --> 00:51:04.869
I mean, some of them are just very, very low.

00:51:04.869 --> 00:51:08.980
BEYLER: So, for instance, Canada, which integrated
their infantry almost 25 years ago?

00:51:08.980 --> 00:51:09.980
SCHAEFER: Yes.

00:51:09.980 --> 00:51:14.130
BEYLER: Twenty-five years ago, and their numbers
hover around the 1 percent range right now.

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:17.579
So, again, but those, right-so that was informative
for us.

00:51:17.579 --> 00:51:21.410
And back to your question about the standards,
so we need to kind of continue the discussion

00:51:21.410 --> 00:51:27.640
about low numbers or no one is fine, right,
because you have to meet the standard.

00:51:27.640 --> 00:51:31.839
And so, right, we have to continue to make
sure that people understand that we are fully

00:51:31.839 --> 00:51:34.700
comfortable with having low numbers.

00:51:34.700 --> 00:51:39.420
DOZIER: So, to play devil's advocate, that
it's interesting you're-Gayle just talked

00:51:39.420 --> 00:51:46.339
about you want to open up all of the force
to look for talent, but then it's only filling

00:51:46.339 --> 00:51:50.660
1 percent, or will probably only fill 1 percent
of those roles.

00:51:50.660 --> 00:51:52.430
BEYLER: Right.

00:51:52.430 --> 00:51:58.410
DOZIER: To play the devil's advocate, it's
a lot of churn and a lot of expense to look

00:51:58.410 --> 00:52:03.160
at this, to open everything up, to get only
1 percent of those roles filled.

00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:08.210
BEYLER: Well, you could say that, or, again,
we don't know who's out there that may-we

00:52:08.210 --> 00:52:09.750
have no idea what it's going to look like.

00:52:09.750 --> 00:52:14.269
So you don't know what those young, young
girls in high school are capable of and wanting

00:52:14.269 --> 00:52:15.480
to do moving forward.

00:52:15.480 --> 00:52:17.609
So we may see increased numbers.

00:52:17.609 --> 00:52:22.259
Again, as we look at training properly, like
better ways to-and fitness, and building that

00:52:22.259 --> 00:52:26.239
body mass, looking at BMI and bone strengthening,
you never know.

00:52:26.239 --> 00:52:28.489
We may actually have more numbers in the United
States.

00:52:28.489 --> 00:52:29.489
LEMMON: It was fascinating.

00:52:29.489 --> 00:52:34.329
So I was at West Point in the fall, and one
of the things that was really fascinating

00:52:34.329 --> 00:52:37.839
there was, you know, you come in and you're
supposed to give this talk.

00:52:37.839 --> 00:52:40.460
And I said, well, I just have a question for
you, you know: how many of you want to go

00:52:40.460 --> 00:52:44.260
into-into infantry and to-and, you know, hands
shot up.

00:52:44.260 --> 00:52:48.599
And just to think that they are on the cusp
of this change, right?

00:52:48.599 --> 00:52:52.260
There are young women at West Point who would
come up to you and say, I've always felt like

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:56.609
there were two tiers: jobs that, you know,
everybody could do, and then jobs that, you

00:52:56.609 --> 00:53:01.079
know, women couldn't, and I have always to
be in infantry.

00:53:01.079 --> 00:53:04.499
There were a couple of the-(chuckles)-young
women who were soldiers in "Ashley's War"

00:53:04.499 --> 00:53:07.640
who actually didn't-enlisted and didn't know
that women couldn't be in the infantry.

00:53:07.640 --> 00:53:12.559
Or ROTC cadets who were, you know, at the
top of their ROTC corps but actually couldn't

00:53:12.559 --> 00:53:13.989
be in infantry when they came out.

00:53:13.989 --> 00:53:19.200
And so, you know, you see all kinds of what
I would call inefficiencies in the system

00:53:19.200 --> 00:53:21.180
in some ways, right, that are being leveled
out.

00:53:21.180 --> 00:53:24.620
But I have never heard anybody talk about
huge numbers, but is there a talent pool out

00:53:24.620 --> 00:53:25.620
there?

00:53:25.620 --> 00:53:26.620
You do see it.

00:53:26.620 --> 00:53:28.329
DOZIER: Well, hey, I went Wellesley.

00:53:28.329 --> 00:53:32.700
I'm just-I'm trying to-(laughter)-this is
not a very skeptical audience, so I'm trying

00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:34.279
to channel the skeptics who are out there.

00:53:34.279 --> 00:53:35.279
LEMMON: No, absolutely.

00:53:35.279 --> 00:53:36.690
And all the questions-all of these questions
come up.

00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:40.640
And I think it's really important to have
discussions about them, because otherwise

00:53:40.640 --> 00:53:46.079
people feel like, well, gosh, you know, they're
just having a conversation divorced from reality.

00:53:46.079 --> 00:53:49.329
And the truth is you just want to have a reflection
of what's already happening.

00:53:49.329 --> 00:53:56.170
SCHAEFER: So, if I could say quickly about
the cost piece, we actually ran a very detailed

00:53:56.170 --> 00:53:59.180
cost analysis in our Marine Corps work.

00:53:59.180 --> 00:54:04.180
And we looked at attrition rates for women
and, you know, how many women you would need

00:54:04.180 --> 00:54:09.029
to bring in to keep the infantry at the same
level it is today, and those kinds of issues,

00:54:09.029 --> 00:54:13.930
and we found that it's less than 1 percent
of the overall personnel budget.

00:54:13.930 --> 00:54:21.609
So, again, this-I think that there's this
misconception that it will be very expensive.

00:54:21.609 --> 00:54:24.549
And that mirrors what the other services have
found, too.

00:54:24.549 --> 00:54:27.979
DOZIER: So less than 1 percent of the overall
personnel budget, what are we talking?

00:54:27.979 --> 00:54:30.529
Because you guys have big budgets.

00:54:30.529 --> 00:54:31.529
(Laughter.)

00:54:31.529 --> 00:54:33.290
SCHAEFER: Yeah, I can't-it was very small.

00:54:33.290 --> 00:54:37.319
I mean, I-sorry, I can't think of the-
DOZIER: Tens of millions?

00:54:37.319 --> 00:54:40.759
SCHAEFER: It was-I think it was-yeah, I'm
not sure.

00:54:40.759 --> 00:54:43.710
But it was-overall, in the scheme it was very
small.

00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:45.690
DOZIER: Very small.

00:54:45.690 --> 00:54:46.999
SCHAEFER: Yeah.

00:54:46.999 --> 00:54:48.319
DOZIER: Sir?

00:54:48.319 --> 00:54:52.769
Q: Damon Porter with the Association-
DOZIER: We can't hear you.

00:54:52.769 --> 00:54:54.970
Q: I'll start again.

00:54:54.970 --> 00:54:57.180
Is it this one?

00:54:57.180 --> 00:54:58.279
Thank you.

00:54:58.279 --> 00:54:59.940
(Comes on mic.)

00:54:59.940 --> 00:55:03.049
Damon Porter with the Association of Global
Automakers.

00:55:03.049 --> 00:55:06.499
We know oftentimes that policy decisions are
not made in a vacuum.

00:55:06.499 --> 00:55:10.849
And clearly there's demonstrable evidence,
as we've discussed today, why women should

00:55:10.849 --> 00:55:12.959
be in combative forces.

00:55:12.959 --> 00:55:15.249
But can we talk a little bit from the historical
perspective?

00:55:15.249 --> 00:55:17.990
We've raised the issues of physical standards.

00:55:17.990 --> 00:55:25.069
How much, such as Title IX, were shaping the
ability of demonstrating the physical dexterity

00:55:25.069 --> 00:55:29.630
and ability of women to serve in combative
roles?

00:55:29.630 --> 00:55:34.049
And I guess the other point to that question
is, how much can women in combative forces

00:55:34.049 --> 00:55:38.739
help shape the policy discussions outside
of armed services, such as equal pay?

00:55:38.739 --> 00:55:42.230
DOZIER: So suddenly we have a whole bunch
of questions with only six minutes left.

00:55:42.230 --> 00:55:43.230
LEMMON: I know.

00:55:43.230 --> 00:55:44.230
(Chuckles, laughter.)

00:55:44.230 --> 00:55:45.230
Always happens.

00:55:45.230 --> 00:55:46.230
DOZIER: So I'm going to ask a couple of people-yeah,
couple of people to do a couple questions

00:55:46.230 --> 00:55:47.230
in a row.

00:55:47.230 --> 00:55:48.230
Can we take your question?

00:55:48.230 --> 00:55:49.230
Q: Hi, yes.

00:55:49.230 --> 00:55:53.630
I'm Sally Adams, and I'm here on behalf of
the Women in Military Service for our America

00:55:53.630 --> 00:55:55.220
Memorial, called WIMSA.

00:55:55.220 --> 00:56:01.209
And we're the only memorial that honors all
women across all services in the nation.

00:56:01.209 --> 00:56:09.920
And in 2014, a DOD report came out and said
that DOD spends about $90 million on 87 military

00:56:09.920 --> 00:56:15.249
service museums, and not one of those is dedicated
to women.

00:56:15.249 --> 00:56:21.529
And I just wanted to know, during this time
of unprecedented progress in the military,

00:56:21.529 --> 00:56:28.539
if the time is ripe for support for this institution
as well as other memorials and museums that

00:56:28.539 --> 00:56:29.539
honor women?

00:56:29.539 --> 00:56:30.539
DOZIER: OK.

00:56:30.539 --> 00:56:31.719
And one more?

00:56:31.719 --> 00:56:33.609
Q: Oh.

00:56:33.609 --> 00:56:36.450
Hi, good morning.

00:56:36.450 --> 00:56:37.450
My name's Christine (sp).

00:56:37.450 --> 00:56:41.910
I'm an undergraduate student at Georgetown
University, but I just transitioned out of

00:56:41.910 --> 00:56:42.910
the Marine Corps.

00:56:42.910 --> 00:56:43.910
I was a corporal.

00:56:43.910 --> 00:56:44.910
I transitioned out in July.

00:56:44.910 --> 00:56:46.880
I was an Arabic linguist.

00:56:46.880 --> 00:56:51.859
And right now at school I do a lot of research
on understanding the Muslim world.

00:56:51.859 --> 00:56:56.519
And we've talked a little bit about the unique
advantage that women bring to the battlefield.

00:56:56.519 --> 00:57:01.089
And, especially as the decision's already
been made for integration, I hope to switch

00:57:01.089 --> 00:57:06.369
the conversation to, like, OK, it's decided
now, so what are we uniquely going to bring

00:57:06.369 --> 00:57:08.029
to this-to this arena.

00:57:08.029 --> 00:57:11.299
So my question is, do you think women will
provide a unique advantage?

00:57:11.299 --> 00:57:15.289
And how can we capitalize on that, specifically
when we're talking about women in encountering

00:57:15.289 --> 00:57:19.819
violent extremism, which I know a lot of NGOs
and think tanks are focusing on?

00:57:19.819 --> 00:57:25.700
And so I'm wondering if the DOD specifically
has looked at how we can integrate women specifically

00:57:25.700 --> 00:57:30.119
to capitalize on women's unique skills in
that arena.

00:57:30.119 --> 00:57:33.430
DOZIER: So we have three questions and three
minutes left.

00:57:33.430 --> 00:57:38.249
Starting with that one, unique skills that
women can provide, are there specific things

00:57:38.249 --> 00:57:42.829
you're recruiting them for within combat roles
that help in those areas?

00:57:42.829 --> 00:57:46.829
BEYLER: I mean, I think-I think that goes
to one of the earlier questions, right?

00:57:46.829 --> 00:57:50.320
So there are-there are-women and men, there
are differences, and so perhaps there are

00:57:50.320 --> 00:57:52.779
the things can women can bring to the discussion.

00:57:52.779 --> 00:57:54.499
And I think it gets to "Ashley's War."

00:57:54.499 --> 00:57:58.989
There were very unique skillsets where the
Special Operations community, you know, realizes

00:57:58.989 --> 00:57:59.989
they need help.

00:57:59.989 --> 00:58:04.150
So this was just really-this was a recognition
of what was actually already going on in the

00:58:04.150 --> 00:58:05.829
fights and in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:58:05.829 --> 00:58:08.940
So, I mean, to answer your question, yes.

00:58:08.940 --> 00:58:11.230
But again, that's part of that entire discussion.

00:58:11.230 --> 00:58:12.690
That was the reason behind all of this.

00:58:12.690 --> 00:58:16.973
We wanted to be able to use the skills of
the women that were out there.

00:58:16.973 --> 00:58:17.973
DOZIER: And the Title IX question?

00:58:17.973 --> 00:58:18.973
Sorry, go ahead.

00:58:18.973 --> 00:58:20.459
LEMMON: Title IX, I think, is fascinating.

00:58:20.459 --> 00:58:25.960
One thing, if-I noticed a couple things that
were across the board on the soldiers who

00:58:25.960 --> 00:58:29.410
were part of "Ashley's War," and the Rangers
were really taking specifically the direct

00:58:29.410 --> 00:58:32.000
actions that were taking, you know, the most
fit.

00:58:32.000 --> 00:58:35.859
And as one Ranger said, if nobody else liked
you, we did.

00:58:35.859 --> 00:58:40.130
You know, they were looking for people who
were ready to go out and be fit and fierce,

00:58:40.130 --> 00:58:43.430
and be able to keep up on those kinds of Special
Operations missions.

00:58:43.430 --> 00:58:45.789
And almost all of them were track athletes.

00:58:45.789 --> 00:58:52.229
Almost all of them had been raised by fathers
who had always treated them the same as their

00:58:52.229 --> 00:58:53.229
brothers.

00:58:53.229 --> 00:58:58.779
Or, if they had no other siblings who were
brothers, they were held to the highest of

00:58:58.779 --> 00:58:59.779
standards.

00:58:59.779 --> 00:59:04.559
And athleticism and sports was very much a
theme across the board.

00:59:04.559 --> 00:59:10.230
And they can talk to you about that more afterward,
but you really did see that across the board

00:59:10.230 --> 00:59:15.489
in terms of always having been fit, always
having trained to a very high standard.

00:59:15.489 --> 00:59:20.910
And Amy, who's here, actually played high
school football all four years.

00:59:20.910 --> 00:59:24.480
She's going to be mortified when I tell you
that-(laughter)-and didn't want to after the

00:59:24.480 --> 00:59:28.779
first couple years, but little girls would
come up to her at games and say that she wanted-they

00:59:28.779 --> 00:59:29.989
wanted to be like her.

00:59:29.989 --> 00:59:32.930
And that-it was sort of hard to quit after
that.

00:59:32.930 --> 00:59:39.420
So, you know, I do think Title IX very much
played a role in the physical athleticism

00:59:39.420 --> 00:59:40.420
and the opportunities.

00:59:40.420 --> 00:59:41.539
DOZIER: You had something to add.

00:59:41.539 --> 00:59:43.009
BEYLER: I wanted to add one quick point.

00:59:43.009 --> 00:59:47.030
I think something that was fascinating for
me is about-you ask, how are these women going

00:59:47.030 --> 00:59:51.029
to affect-a lot of the senior men that were
involved in this conversation, right, they

00:59:51.029 --> 00:59:52.499
have daughters.

00:59:52.499 --> 00:59:59.140
And a lot of their thinking on this issue
was formed by those daughters and what they

00:59:59.140 --> 01:00:00.809
wanted to see, you know?

01:00:00.809 --> 01:00:03.789
Their daughters were phenomenal, and why shouldn't
my daughter be able to do this?

01:00:03.789 --> 01:00:07.390
And many of those daughters are serving in
uniform as young captains and lieutenants

01:00:07.390 --> 01:00:08.390
now.

01:00:08.390 --> 01:00:10.460
So I absolutely think-and they may not be
on the cusp.

01:00:10.460 --> 01:00:14.539
They may not be, but they are out there and
they are absolutely the ones that continue

01:00:14.539 --> 01:00:15.869
to inform this discussion.

01:00:15.869 --> 01:00:18.400
I think that's fascinating about all of the
dads.

01:00:18.400 --> 01:00:27.000
DOZIER: And last quick point, the women institutions
or museums memorializing women in the military.

01:00:27.000 --> 01:00:28.000
BEYLER: Right.

01:00:28.000 --> 01:00:31.020
So, absolutely, WIMSA does some phenomenal
work, and we thank you for what you do.

01:00:31.020 --> 01:00:35.339
I can't exactly, but budgets are tight and
we certainly support the work that you do.

01:00:35.339 --> 01:00:36.339
It's fantastic.

01:00:36.339 --> 01:00:37.339
Q: Thank you.

01:00:37.339 --> 01:00:38.339
DOZIER: Thank you very much.

01:00:38.339 --> 01:00:42.859
I want to thank the panelists for answering
some tough questions and showing the public

01:00:42.859 --> 01:00:50.559
out there that the tough questions have been
asked before enacting this policy.

01:00:50.559 --> 01:00:54.839
And thank you all for attending this session
on "Women in the Armed Forces: The Future

01:00:54.839 --> 01:00:56.059
of the Military."

01:00:56.059 --> 01:01:16.019
That concludes this CFR session.

01:01:16.019 --> 01:01:22.420
(Applause.)

01:01:22.420 --> 01:01:28.819
(END)

